{"query":"What has David Nelsen published on Tyrannosaurus rex?","response":{"answer":"**David Nelsen’s published work on *Tyrannosaurus rex***  \n\n| Publication | Key focus on *T. rex* | Co‑authors | Source |\n|-------------|-----------------------|------------|--------|\n| *Tyrannosaur cannibalism: A case of a tooth‑traced tyrannosaurid bone in the Lance Formation (Maastrichtian), Wyoming* | Presents a partial right metatarsal IV (SWAU HRS13997) that bears tooth‑score traces matching the denticle width of *T. rex* teeth, interpreted as evidence of cannibalistic feeding by a *T. rex* on a smaller tyrannosaurid. The paper discusses the morphology, histology, and taphonomy of the bone, and situates the find within the broader context of *T. rex* cannibalism and feeding behavior. | Matthew A. McLain, David Nelsen, Keith Snyder, Christopher T. Griffin, Bethania Siviero, Leonard R. Brand & Arthur V. Chadwick | Document 1 |\n\n**Summary**\n\nDavid Nelsen is a co‑author of the above study, which is the only publication listed in the provided documents that directly addresses *Tyrannosaurus rex*. The paper contributes to our understanding of *T. rex* cannibalism by documenting tooth‑score traces on a tyrannosaurid metatarsal that are best explained by a *T. rex* feeding on a smaller individual.","model":"gpt-oss:20b-cloud","treatments":[{"treatments_id":19909,"treatmentId":"038466034D5FDE59FBD84DEDFA16F78C","zenodoDep":3749641,"treatmentTitle":"Tyrannosaurus rex Osborn 1905","treatmentAuthor":"Matthew A. McLain","articleTitle":"Tyrannosaur cannibalism: A case of a tooth-traced tyrannosaurid bone in the Lance Formation (Maastrichtian), Wyoming","articleAuthor":"Matthew A. McLain, David Nelsen, Keith Snyder, Christopher T. Griffin, Bethania Siviero, Leonard R. Brand & Arthur V. Chadwick","journalYear":2018,"journalTitle":"Palaios","publicationDate":"","status":"","genus":"Tyrannosaurus","species":"rex","score":0.567551858549645,"images":[{"httpUri":"https://zenodo.org/record/3749643/files/figure.png","captionText":"FIG. 1.—A Tyrannosaurus rex metatarsal IV (SWAU HRS13997) in four views. Scale bar = 100 mm."},{"httpUri":"https://zenodo.org/record/3749645/files/figure.png","captionText":"FIG. 2.—Tooth traces on SWAU HRS13997. The two sets of Knethichnus paralleum and Linichnus serratus are indicated, with the more prominent pair on the right. Scale bar = 20 mm."},{"httpUri":"https://zenodo.org/record/3749647/files/figure.png","captionText":"FIG. 3.—Details of Knethichnus parallelum and Linichnus serratus traces on SWAU HRS13997. A) Most prominent K. parallelum (center) and L. serratus (bottom) examples. B) Most prominent L. serratus in more detail and a different lighting angle. Small portion of the most prominent K. parallelum is also visible in the lower right corner. C) Detailed view of the other, less prominent L. serratus (left) and K. parallelum (right) traces. All scale bars = 1 mm."},{"httpUri":"https://zenodo.org/record/4023994/files/figure.png","captionText":"FIG. 4.—Histology of a Tyrannosaurus rex metatarsal (SWAU HRS13997) in cross section under different light regimes. A) Section of cortical bone from the medullary cavity (left) to the subperiosteal surface (right) under regularly transmitted light. Boxes show where areas of this bone are shown in higher detail under regularly transmitted light (B, F, J), cross-polarized light (C, G, K), cross-polarized light with a waveplate (D, H, L), and reflected light (E, I, M). Scale bar for A = 5 mm, all other scale bars = 1 mm."},{"httpUri":"https://zenodo.org/record/3749649/files/figure.png","captionText":"FIG. 5.—Comparison between four specimens of tyrannosaurid metatarsals: A) Tyrannosaurus rex metatarsal IV (SWAU HRS13997). B) Tyrannosaurus rex metatarsals II-IV (LACM 23845, with IV sculpted). C) Tyrannosauridae indet. metatarsal IV (IGM 6130; modified from Peecook (2014, fig. 3). D) Tyrannosaurus rex metatarsals II-IV (FMNH PR2081; modified from Brochu (2003, figs. 99, 100, 101). SWAU HRS13997 and IGM 6130 are metatarsal IV. All elements are from the right pes except IGM 6130, which is a left element, although the image has been flipped to make it match the others. Note that FMNH PR2081 is shown in a more medial view than the other metatarsal specimens. Scale bar = 100 mm."},{"httpUri":"https://zenodo.org/record/3740185/files/figure.png","captionText":"Brochu 2003: FIGURE 101. FMNH PR2081, Tyrannosaurus rex. Right fourth metatarsal (MTIV) in medial (A), lateral (B), dorsal (C), and ventral (D) view. Scale =10 cm. Photographs by J. Weinstein. "}]}]},"stored":1782579401808,"ttl":-1,"embedding":[-0.07069641351699829,0.024672169238328934,0.021651964634656906,-0.01908082328736782,0.006757095456123352,0.014946195296943188,-0.01381145790219307,0.04958365112543106,-0.025718171149492264,-0.04130437970161438,0.005875895265489817,-0.027711886912584305,0.03908697888255119,0.05947759374976158,-0.0122013995423913,-0.0343424491584301,0.032455846667289734,0.02612384594976902,-0.033639222383499146,0.06480760872364044,0.05973503366112709,-0.037916842848062515,0.010171424597501755,-0.06727806478738785,0.05459946021437645,0.03349227458238602,-0.05143513157963753,0.015804853290319443,-0.05845744162797928,-0.2329423576593399,0.03789317607879639,-0.062069375067949295,-0.031995613127946854,-0.02913963422179222,0.009989907033741474,-0.004008366260677576,-0.0304384995251894,0.015465294010937214,0.027531005442142487,0.05388767644762993,0.027935054153203964,0.032365310937166214,-0.022217053920030594,-0.09348483383655548,-0.034082457423210144,-0.027375122532248497,-0.050914499908685684,-0.07590317726135254,0.050080060958862305,-0.016370093449950218,-0.029176201671361923,-0.04535311833024025,0.0241677425801754,0.04569675773382187,0.05153370276093483,0.02297040820121765,0.055303722620010376,0.02450658194720745,0.029066968709230423,0.0076194042339921,0.055475711822509766,0.035740699619054794,-0.2507152259349823,0.08009670674800873,0.07456657290458679,0.034252993762493